::ENG3_U06_L07_GP01::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is preparing a LAN publication that includes writing, a chart, and a short oral introduction.
Which final check best shows publication readiness?{
~The chart is colorful even if it is unrelated.#Incorrect. Color does not show that the chart supports the audience or purpose.
=The writing, chart, and introduction all support the same audience and purpose.#Correct. Publication-ready multimedia communication requires the parts to work together for the same readers.
~The oral introduction is longer than the written summary.#Incorrect. Length does not prove quality or audience fit.
~The page has several decorative images.#Incorrect. Decoration alone does not make the product publication ready.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_GP02::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student wants to publish a research summary online.
What should be revised first?{
~The background color only#Incorrect. Color is a design detail, not the first content-readiness issue.
~The number of images only#Incorrect. Multimedia matters, but the writing and purpose must be clear first.
~The export file name only#Incorrect. File naming is a final check, not the first revision priority.
=The clarity of the claim, evidence, and explanation for the audience#Correct. The writing must communicate clearly before the product can be publication ready.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_GP03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A slide includes a useful timeline, but the published summary never mentions it.
What is the best correction?{
=Add a sentence or caption that connects the timeline to the written claim.#Correct. Multimedia should be integrated with the writing so readers understand why it matters.
~Delete the written summary and keep only the timeline.#Incorrect. Publication-ready communication still needs clear writing.
~Add another timeline without explanation.#Incorrect. More media does not fix the missing connection.
~Make the timeline smaller so readers ignore it.#Incorrect. Hiding the problem does not improve communication.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_GP04::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is preparing to export a final multimedia product.
Which item belongs on the final checklist?{
~Whether every slide uses a different font#Incorrect. Different fonts may reduce readability and do not prove quality.
~Whether the product is longer than a classmate's work#Incorrect. Length does not measure publication readiness.
=Whether writing, media, sources, and conventions are clear for the intended audience#Correct. This checklist covers the main features of publication-ready communication.
~Whether every blank space is filled#Incorrect. Filling space can clutter the product and weaken audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L07_GP05::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student says, "My product is done because all parts are finished."
What feedback is strongest?{
~Finished parts always mean the product is publication ready.#Incorrect. Parts may be finished but still disconnected.
=Check whether the parts work together for one audience and one purpose.#Correct. Integration is necessary before a multimedia product is ready to publish.
~Delete the multimedia so there is less to check.#Incorrect. Purposeful multimedia can strengthen the product.
~Publish immediately before revising.#Incorrect. Publication should happen after revision and final checks.
}
